From b74aaebd17b3956e9733cb3a46547abb15650cf6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Rakibul Islam <33988342+raykibul@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 3 Sep 2026 00:56:47 +0600
Subject: [PATCH 4/8] feat(desktop): let the new-session dialog take files, as
 the chat composer does
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The dialog reads as the same control as the session prompt, so a
screenshot dragged onto it should land on it. Until now nothing
happened: no ⊕, no drop target, no paste.

It is one composer's worth of behaviour used twice, so it moves out of
chat.tsx rather than being written a second time. attach.tsx holds the
hook that owns the files, the drop target, the ⊕ and its hidden input,
the chips and the paste offer; chat.tsx now uses those and is 140 lines
shorter for it. The terminal's own drop is left alone — it types paths
into a pty and has no chips to share.

The one part that is not the chat composer: POST /api/sessions/{id}/files
needs a session that exists, and in this dialog none does yet. So the
files are held in memory, the session is created silent, the upload goes
against the id it hands back, and the prompt naming the paths is sent as
the first turn. An upload that fails after that point does not undo the
session — it is running, and killing an agent over an attachment is the
worse answer — so the prompt goes without the paths and the failure is
said out loud.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
